CAT = "iiqeEthics"; SEG = "ETH"
Q = [
("In law, an insurance broker is normally the agent of the:",
 "Policyholder", ["Insurer", "Insurance Authority", "Reinsurer"],
 "A broker acts for the client, owes agency duties to the insured and is expected to give impartial, expert advice independent of any one insurer."),
("A client's personal data must be used only for the purpose it was collected for, or a directly related one, unless the client consents. This is data protection principle:",
 "3, use of personal data", ["1, purpose and manner of collection", "4, security of personal data", "6, access to personal data"],
 "Principle 3 limits use to the collection purpose or a directly related purpose unless the data subject gives prescribed consent."),
("When collecting a customer's personal data, an insurer should give a statement of purpose, transferees and access rights known as a:",
 "Personal Information Collection Statement", ["Privacy Policy Statement", "Suspicious Transaction Report", "Client agreement with terms of business"],
 "Under principle 1, a PICS attached to the application form tells customers the purpose, possible transferees, the effect of not supplying data and their access rights."),
("Before passing a client's personal data to another company for its direct marketing, a data user must receive from the client:",
 "Written consent or no objection", ["Oral consent only, on the phone", "No reply within 14 days", "Consent from the IA"],
 "Providing data to others for direct marketing needs a written reply showing consent or no objection, after the prescribed information is given in writing."),
("Under Hong Kong's anti-discrimination law, an insurer may charge women a lower life premium than men of the same age where:",
 "Reliable data supports it", ["The agent agrees to it", "The client asks for it", "It is never allowed"],
 "The Ordinances allow different treatment in insurance based on reliable actuarial or other data where it is reasonable; women's longer life expectancy is such data."),
("Which is an example of unfair discrimination in insurance?",
 "Refusing cover to a divorced woman", ["Rating by reliable mortality data", "Charging more for a risky occupation", "Declining a proven high risk"],
 "Refusing household cover because a woman is divorced or a single parent is unfair discrimination; risk-based treatment backed by data is legitimate."),
("A launderer buys a single premium policy with cash and soon surrenders it for a cheque from the insurer. This stage is:",
 "Layering", ["Placement", "Integration", "Structuring"],
 "Buying insurance and withdrawing the funds as a 'clean' cheque from the insurer separates proceeds from their source, the layering stage."),
("An agent suspects a client's premium comes from crime. The report is ultimately made to the:",
 "Joint Financial Intelligence Unit", ["Insurance Complaints Bureau", "Privacy Commissioner", "Equal Opportunities Commission"],
 "A suspicious transaction report goes to the JFIU of the Hong Kong Police Force, usually through the firm's Money Laundering Reporting Officer."),
("After reporting a suspicion, an agent tells the client that an investigation may follow. The agent has committed the offence of:",
 "Tipping off", ["Layering", "Ratification", "Contribution"],
 "Disclosing anything likely to prejudice an investigation, knowing or suspecting a report has been made, is the offence of tipping off."),
("Before taking on a customer who is a non-Hong Kong politically exposed person, an insurance institution must obtain:",
 "Senior management approval", ["The IA's prior consent", "A court order in Hong Kong", "The JFIU's clearance"],
 "Enhanced due diligence for a non-Hong Kong PEP requires senior management approval and reasonable steps to establish source of wealth and funds."),
("Under the Prevention of Bribery Ordinance, which of these counts as an 'advantage'?",
 "A cash red packet", ["A meal eaten on the spot", "A client's thank you note", "A coffee at a meeting"],
 "'Advantage' covers money, gifts, loans, commissions and favours, including red packet and tea money; entertainment (food and drink consumed on the spot) is excluded."),
("A client offers an agent money to help hide a pre-existing illness on a life application. Under the POBO:",
 "Both client and agent commit offences", ["Only the agent commits an offence", "Only the client commits an offence", "No offence if the illness is minor"],
 "Section 9 makes both soliciting or accepting, and offering, an advantage without the principal's permission an offence."),
]
